Hence, the $2 \mu \mathrm{F}$ capacitor can be neglected.
The equivalent circuit is given by the figure below<br><img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fcomedk%252FAPGarSRYoERq425ghgFqgfL7v117KT_2UvEE2FrwHNw.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fcomedk%2FAPGarSRYoERq425ghgFqgfL7v117KT_2UvEE2FrwHNw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fcomedk%2FAPGarSRYoERq425ghgFqgfL7v117KT_2UvEE2FrwHNw.original.fullsize.png?alt=media"><br>
In the upper arm capacitors $3 \mu \mathrm{F}$ and $6 \mu \mathrm{F}$ are connected in series and similarly in lower arm capacitors $4 \mu \mathrm{F}$ and $8 \mu \mathrm{F}$ are connected in series. The equivalent capacitance is given by $\begin{aligned}\frac{1}{C_{1}}&=\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{6}=\frac{2+1}{6}=\frac{3}{6}\\
\Rightarrow \quad C_{1} &=2 \mu \mathrm{F}
\end{aligned} \begin{array}{ll}
\text{ and }& \frac{1}{C_{2}}=\frac{1}{4}+\frac{1}{8}=\frac{2+1}{8}=\frac{3}{8}\\
\Rightarrow \quad C_{2} & =\frac{8}{3} \mu \overrightarrow{\mathrm{F}}
\end{array}$ The reduced circuit with $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$ capacitance is given as \\ $<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fcomedk%252FIzT20Bpx1bshbUlWG2YPn0XcbVUcULx9FjONNzCBGgk.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia \text{ and } clean=1 \text{ and } v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fcomedk%2FIzT20Bpx1bshbUlWG2YPn0XcbVUcULx9FjONNzCBGgk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fcomedk%2FIzT20Bpx1bshbUlWG2YPn0XcbVUcULx9FjONNzCBGgk.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ Now, $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$ are connected in parallel combination. Hence, equivalent capacitance is given by $\begin{aligned}&C_{\mathrm{eq}}=C_{1}+C_{2} \\
&C_{e q}=2+\frac{8}{3}=\frac{14}{3} \mu \mathrm{F}
\end{aligned}$
